{"kind":"report","text":"Assessment of 6aea2041: close the branch, and revive one test from it. The guard is shipped and held; the residual value is a single case main does not cover, and it is sharper than the branch it came from.\n\nTHE GUARD IS ALREADY ON MAIN. internal/workroom/fold.go:516 reads if record.index \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 !f.hasActor(record.record.Actor), refusing with statement author is not a live participant. bbee4a4d adds that same line verbatim. It landed in 8525b30e, and the comment above it states the purpose the branch was for: the gap between holding a key and holding authority. So the request's premise -- planner found no live-membership marker in main fold.go -- is wrong, and I checked that before claiming rather than reporting it after doing the work.\n\nAND IT IS HELD, which is the question that actually matters. Disabling the guard on main turns three tests red: TestADepartedParticipantCannotAuthorState, TestADepartedPerformerCannotFileAReport/filed_after_removal, and TestRestoredMembershipRestoresAuthorship. A shipped guard with no test is a guard that survives until someone deletes it; this one is not that.\n\nTHE ONE THING THE BRANCH STILL HAS. internal/app was untouched by that mutation, and bbee4a4d adds 79 lines there: TestHistoricalCheckpointReplayAppliesLiveMembershipProfile, absent from main. Main has TestProjectionProfileChangeRebuildsFromTheSameKernelCheckpoint, and the two are complementary rather than duplicate. Main asserts that a profile change invalidates the cache, rebuilds from the signed kernel checkpoint, and that a profile-only rebuild leaves the projection unchanged. The dormant one asserts the other half: when the rules genuinely changed, a checkpoint from the preceding fold is rejected by profile, all signed history is replayed, and the projection is republished under the new rules -- a different answer, not the same one.\n\nWHY THAT MATTERS RIGHT NOW. My in-review head 8b01caca advances ProfileVersion to workroom-fold@8 precisely because three historical decisions change on a re-fold. Main has no test that a profile bump with changed rules produces the new answer, which is exactly the case that lane relies on. Reviving that one test is worth more than reviving the branch, and it has somewhere real to point.\n\nRECOMMENDATION. Retire request/live-membership-authority-v2 and its branch: everything in it except that test is on main. File a child request for the app-level replay test, and if you agree, the natural home is the workroom-fold@8 lane rather than a branch of its own, since that is the first profile bump with genuinely changed rules to exercise it. I have not created that child or touched the branch -- both are yours to direct, and the branch is not mine to delete."}